<exercisegroup cols="2" xml:id="exercisegroup-orthog-normal-plane-eqn-R3">
<title>Normal vector to a plane in <m> \R^3 </m> from its equation</title>

<introduction><p>
	In each of the following,
	a linear equation corresponding to a plane in <m> \R^3 </m> and two points <m> P </m> and <m> Q </m> are provided.
	<ol>

		<li xml:id="instruction-exercisegroup-orthog-normal-plane-eqn-R3-normal-vec">
		By inspection,
		determine a <term><xref ref="definition-orthog-normal-vector" text="custom">normal vector</xref></term> for the plane from its equation.
		</li>

		<li> Verify that points <m> P </m> and <m> Q </m> lie on the plane. </li>

		<li>
		Compute the vector <m> \uvec{v} </m> associated to directed line segment <m> \abray{PQ} </m>,
		and then verify that <m> \uvec{v} </m> is orthogonal to your normal vector from <xref ref="instruction-exercisegroup-orthog-normal-plane-eqn-R3-normal-vec" text="type-local">part</xref>.
		</li>

	</ol>
</p></introduction>

<exercise>

	<statement>

		<p><m> \Pi \colon \:\: 5 x + 3 y - 5 z = 2 </m></p>

		<p><m> P(1,-1,0), Q(-1,-1,-2) </m></p>

	</statement>

	<answer><p><ol>

		<li><p> The plane equation coefficients form a normal vector: <md> \uvec{n} = (5,3,-5) </md>. </p></li>

		<li><p><md>

			<mrow> P \amp \colon \:\: 5 \cdot 1 + 3 \cdot (-1) - 5 \cdot 0 = 2 </mrow>

			<mrow> Q \amp \colon \:\: 5 \cdot (-1) + 3 \cdot (-1) - 5 \cdot (-2) = 2 </mrow>

		</md></p></li>

		<li><p>
			<m> \uvec{v} = (-2,0,-2) </m>
			<md> \udotprod{n}{v} = 5 \cdot (-2) + 3 \cdot 0 + (-5) \cdot (-2) = 0 </md>,
			hence orthogonal.
		</p></li>

	</ol></p></answer>

</exercise>

<exercise>

	<statement>

		<p><m> \Pi \colon \:\: 4 x - 5 y + z = -2 </m></p>

		<p><m> P(1,1,-1), Q(4,3,-3) </m></p>

	</statement>

	<answer><p><ol>

		<li><p> The plane equation coefficients form a normal vector: <md> \uvec{n} = (4,-5,1) </md>. </p></li>

		<li><p><md>

			<mrow> P \amp \colon \:\: 4 \cdot 1 - 5 \cdot 1 + (-1) = -2 </mrow>

			<mrow> Q \amp \colon \:\: 4 \cdot 4 - 5 \cdot 3 + (-3) = -2 </mrow>

		</md></p></li>

		<li><p>
			<m> \uvec{v} = (3,2,-2) </m>
			<md> \udotprod{n}{v} = 4 \cdot 3 + (-5) \cdot 2 + 1 \cdot (-2) = 0 </md>,
			hence orthogonal.
		</p></li>

	</ol></p></answer>

</exercise>

</exercisegroup>
